The basic Git stash command is:

```bash id="g3b5pk"
git stash
```

This temporarily saves your uncommitted changes and restores your working directory to the last commit.

### Common stash commands

* Save changes with a message:

```bash id="8p36d6"
git stash push -m "work in progress"
```

* View stashes:

```bash id="b0j1vk"
git stash list
```

* Apply the latest stash (keep it in stash list):

```bash id="0f4u3w"
git stash apply
```

* Apply and remove the latest stash:

```bash id="8x8o9i"
git stash pop
```

* Apply a specific stash:

```bash id="2v9x8j"
git stash apply stash@{1}
```

* Delete a specific stash:

```bash id="rrdfm4"
git stash drop stash@{0}
```

* Delete all stashes:

```bash id="zv7j5z"
git stash clear
```

* Stash including untracked files:

```bash id="v9cnlz"
git stash -u
```

* Stash only staged changes:

```bash id="z64rc7"
git stash --staged
```

### Typical workflow

```bash id="9x4y3r"
git status
git stash
git pull
git stash pop
```

This is commonly used when you need to quickly switch branches or pull updates without committing unfinished work.
